"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,435\nEpisode 12\n\n3\n00:00:07,515 --> 00:00:09,575\nErrors and feedback\n\n4\n00:00:12,375 --> 00:00:13,705\nGuess before you know.\n\n5\n00:00:14,081 --> 00:00:18,546\nIn one study, students tried to answer questions before they had learned the answers,\n\n6\n00:00:18,748 --> 00:00:20,393\nthen saw the correct answer.\n\n7\n00:00:20,863 --> 00:00:23,166\nTheir guesses were almost always wrong.\n\n8\n00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:27,534\nYet they remembered the answers better than students who simply studied them.\n\n9\n00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:30,852\nA wrong guess, followed by the right answer,\n\n10\n00:00:30,998 --> 00:00:32,190\nis a learning event.\n\n11\n00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:34,002\nRecall episode four.\n\n12\n00:00:34,427 --> 00:00:38,145\nThe brain learns from the gap between what it expected and what happened.\n\n13\n00:00:38,588 --> 00:00:40,802\nAn error is that gap made visible.\n\n14\n00:00:41,052 --> 00:00:44,187\nAnd confident errors are not the hardest to fix.\n\n15\n00:00:44,551 --> 00:00:47,860\nPeople are more likely to correct an error they were sure about,\n\n16\n00:00:47,947 --> 00:00:49,308\nonce they see the answer.\n\n17\n00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:51,836\nIt's called the hypercorrection effect.\n\n18\n00:00:52,006 --> 00:00:53,870\nThis scales up to whole lessons.\n\n19\n00:00:54,149 --> 00:00:58,577\nIn productive failure, students wrestle with a problem before they are taught the method,\n\n20\n00:00:58,770 --> 00:01:00,231\nand usually fall short.\n\n21\n00:01:00,701 --> 00:01:02,355\nThen instruction follows.\n\n22\n00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:08,119\nA meta-analysis of fifty-three studies found that this order generally beat instruction first,\n\n23\n00:01:08,198 --> 00:01:09,982\nfor conceptual understanding.\n\n24\n00:01:10,392 --> 00:01:11,338\nBut not always.\n\n25\n00:01:11,358 --> 00:01:14,455\nYounger students, and some general skills,\n\n26\n00:01:14,590 --> 00:01:16,643\ndid better with instruction first.\n\n27\n00:01:16,812 --> 00:01:19,151\nErrors only help when feedback follows.\n\n28\n00:01:19,482 --> 00:01:25,042\nWithout it, a wrong option chosen on a multiple-choice test can be learned as if it were true.\n\n29\n00:01:25,452 --> 00:01:27,620\nWith feedback, those errors drop.\n\n30\n00:01:28,018 --> 00:01:29,647\nAnd not all feedback helps.\n\n31\n00:01:29,967 --> 00:01:31,716\nIn a large meta-analysis,\n\n32\n00:01:31,807 --> 00:01:34,405\nfeedback improved performance on average,\n\n33\n00:01:34,667 --> 00:01:38,634\nyet more than a third of feedback interventions made performance worse,\n\n34\n00:01:38,890 --> 00:01:42,966\nespecially when feedback drew attention to the person instead of the task.\n\n35\n00:01:43,136 --> 00:01:43,942\nFor teaching.\n\n36\n00:01:44,190 --> 00:01:47,380\nAsk students to predict or attempt before you explain.\n\n37\n00:01:47,773 --> 00:01:49,044\nMake errors cheap: ungraded,\n\n38\n00:01:49,226 --> 00:01:51,377\nprivate, and expected.\n\n39\n00:01:51,713 --> 00:01:53,377\nAsk how confident they are,\n\n40\n00:01:53,397 --> 00:01:55,687\nso surprising errors get attention.\n\n41\n00:01:56,109 --> 00:01:58,630\nFollow every attempt with clear feedback,\n\n42\n00:01:58,650 --> 00:01:59,118\nfast.\n\n43\n00:01:59,457 --> 00:02:00,402\nAim it at the work,\n\n44\n00:02:00,422 --> 00:02:01,313\nnot the person.\n\n45\n00:02:01,588 --> 00:02:03,106\nThen let them try again.\n\n46\n00:02:03,276 --> 00:02:06,435\nAn error is a signal that the model needs updating.\n\n47\n00:02:06,804 --> 00:02:09,086\nWith feedback, it becomes learning.\n\n"
